1
00:00:05,180 --> 00:00:13,100
ADB or Android Debug Bridge is a command line tool that lets you communicate with an Android device.

2
00:00:13,100 --> 00:00:19,880
So in fact, ADB is the most popular command line tool which we use when working with Android.

3
00:00:19,910 --> 00:00:26,780
The ADB command facilitates a variety of device actions such as installing and debugging apps, and

4
00:00:26,780 --> 00:00:33,540
it also provides access to a unique shell that you can use to run a variety of commands on a device.

5
00:00:33,560 --> 00:00:38,060
It's a client server program that mainly includes three components.

6
00:00:38,060 --> 00:00:41,020
So it has a client which sends the command.

7
00:00:41,030 --> 00:00:44,060
The client runs on your development machine.

8
00:00:44,060 --> 00:00:51,680
You can invoke a client from a command line terminal by issuing an ADB command, so you can invoke the

9
00:00:51,680 --> 00:00:54,930
client by using this ADB command.

10
00:00:54,950 --> 00:01:01,580
Once you have installed ADB in your machine and configured the path correctly for the command line terminal,

11
00:01:01,580 --> 00:01:04,130
then you can use the ADB directly like this.

12
00:01:04,580 --> 00:01:08,210
So the ADB command will be available in the terminal.

13
00:01:08,240 --> 00:01:11,660
The second component is a daimon ADB.

14
00:01:12,110 --> 00:01:13,640
So ADB daimon.

15
00:01:13,640 --> 00:01:16,190
This runs commands on a device.

16
00:01:16,190 --> 00:01:20,330
Daimon runs as a background process on each device.

17
00:01:20,420 --> 00:01:27,080
For example, I have the emulator running here and in the command line I get into ADB Shell.

18
00:01:27,080 --> 00:01:31,310
So I'm basically getting into the shell of this emulator.

19
00:01:31,550 --> 00:01:35,580
And within the emulator I can run all the shell commands.

20
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:41,450
We can use the PS command to check whether the ADB is running in the emulator.

21
00:01:41,450 --> 00:01:48,590
So you can just simply grep by ADB, the ADB layman, and then you can see that it's running or five

22
00:01:48,590 --> 00:01:48,970
five.

23
00:01:48,980 --> 00:01:50,660
That's the process ID.

24
00:01:50,690 --> 00:01:57,290
The third component is a server which manages communication between the client and Daemon.

25
00:01:57,320 --> 00:02:02,060
The server runs as a background process on your development mission.

26
00:02:02,090 --> 00:02:06,500
ADB is included in the Android SDK Platform Tools package.

27
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:13,850
You can download this package with the SDK manager, which will install it under the SDK platform tools,

28
00:02:13,850 --> 00:02:16,700
or you can even download it with aosp.

29
00:02:16,700 --> 00:02:22,670
So if you have aosp already downloaded after building, it will be available under the out folder so

30
00:02:22,670 --> 00:02:29,930
you can find ADB command or the ADB executable under Android SDK SDK platform tools.

31
00:02:29,930 --> 00:02:36,290
So once you download the SDK, it's already there and you could just set this part in the command line

32
00:02:36,290 --> 00:02:39,170
and then you can directly use ADB.

33
00:02:39,620 --> 00:02:48,110
And in case if you have already downloaded the AOSP, then you have it under out host Linux x86 and

34
00:02:48,110 --> 00:02:51,830
the build and then under Bing you get the ADB.

35
00:02:52,190 --> 00:02:54,440
So you can use either of these.

36
00:02:54,440 --> 00:02:59,390
If you have the SDK, you can use that or with Aosp, you can make use of this one.

